ctured log field convention (name, namespace, resourceKind), camelCase rationale, and three-problem diagnosis, and publishes it for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator per COO).\n\nScope (content outline inside Scope):\n- Contribution guideline specifying: required fields (name, namespace, resourceKind), camelCase naming rationale, before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, DSCI key migration guide with before/after query examples, the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind), cluster-scoped resource handling\n- Publish the convention as a specification for external operator adoption per platform policy (overlay 0008 — voluntary, RHOAI does not mandate)\n- File tracking issues in COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) per risk mitigation\n- Release-notes documentation of the DSCI key migration\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- The contribution guideline exists, specifying required fields, camelCase rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (strategy AC 7)\n- Guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented logging changes (Rule 24)\n- DSCI migration documented in release notes with before/after query examples\n- Convention published and tracking issues filed in the external operator projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator)\n\nHmm, \"tracking issues filed in COO projects\" — filing issues in external projects is the epic's own deliverable action (filing, not their adoption). OK as AC.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-8 (P1).\n\nRule 23 (rollback/feature-flag): strategy replaces existing capability? Log format change — not a capability replacement. The NFR handles backwards compat. Not applying Rule 23. Rule 25 (konflux): no konflux-onboarding in chain. Not applicable — wait, rhods-operator builds with Dockerfile.konflux already; no onboarding epic needed (component in platform). Fine.\n\nNow — priority check for validator