ule controller action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) are in scope per SME confirmation that they contain error logs.\n2. **Controller architecture corrected to 5 tiers**: DSC controller, 8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI controller, 5 service controllers (including Setup). The AI text's \"four controller tiers\" omitted the module controller tier.\n3. **camelCase field naming** (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) adopted per SME specification.\n4. **Cloudmanager** confirmed to use the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger — only embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind` are needed there.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator manager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document the structured reconciliation log convention and publish for external adoption | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 manager binary log standardization] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 convention guideline]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 cloudmanager log standardization] --> E003\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics — the guideline documents the convention as implemented, so it must follow the code work. Docs edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same — the guideline also covers the cloudmanager controllers' convention. |\n| (no edge) E001 ↔ E002 | Rules 14/15 | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries in the same repo with no shared artifacts — different areas, no build-against relationship. The conversion in each binary is independent; merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. They run in parallel. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1: Convert message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001 | Manager-binary call sites; cloudmanager embedded-identity sites are converted in E002 (same HLR, binary split). |\n| HLR-2: DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller. |\n| HLR-3: Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-4: All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-5: Cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-6: Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n| HLR-7: DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E001 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: it edits the very same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR-1/HLR-4 conversion in the same controllers. Splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit; the `resourceKind` field is added in the same mechanical pass. |\n| HLR-8: Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Open Questions Disposition\n\nNo Investigation epics were created — none of the strategy's open questions changes which epics exist or what they do:\n\n- **Additional fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (PM / Observability): additive to the same call sites; captured as a decision point on the manager implementation epic. Does not change approach.\n- **Which alerts rely on message-string patterns** (SRE / Support): resolved by the log-consumer regression validation step — an acceptance criterion on the implementation epics, not a structural unknown.\n- **Cloudmanager same-release vs. subsequent release** (PM / ODH Operator team): scheduling, not structure. Cloudmanager is a separate epic (E002) precisely so it can be scheduled independently; the work exists either way.\n- **Actual percentage of embedded-identity log calls** (ODH Operator team): the audit is the first step of the implementation epics' scope; the conversion proceeds over whatever sites are found.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Epic size overage (E001), kept deliberately**: The manager-binary scope (audit + conversion + DSCI migration + `resourceKind` across 5 controller tiers and 15 action files + CI lint rule) is estimated at 1.25–1.5 sprints, above the ~2-week guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is a single mechanical pass over one surface (the `log.Error` call sites) that shares one audit; splitting would duplicate the audit and fragment the pass. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along controller-tier lines (e.g., controllers vs. action files, or the DSCI key migration as its own epic) if the call-site audit finds materially more embedded-identity sites than the 75% SME estimate implies, or discovers reconciliation error paths beyond the enumerated controllers and 15 action files.\n- **Pending process gates**: all strategy-level prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support, etc.) are marked \"Pending human review\". These gate execution start, not decomposition structure; the implementing team should not begin until they clear.\n- **Priority bundling**: HLR-7 (P1) is bundled into the P0 manager epic — see the traceability row above for the inline rationale (same call sites, same mechanical pass).\n- **External adoption is voluntary**: COO team adoption of the convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is out of RHOAI's control per platform policy (overlay 0008). Publishing and filing tracking issues is in scope (E003); adoption itself is best-effort and appears in no epic's acceptance criteria.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO adoption) is voluntary and non-blocking by platform policy — no downstream work is gated on it, so there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and already exists.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":47473,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bcd7d7142debc9e","agent_id":"acc070392a2e429ac","session_id":"a719d0dc-396c-46d9-8e0e-5a6d9af711e0","uuid":"c75a5865-56a3-45cc-a4ba-3994152e50d7","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:17:52.801Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
