{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9961993c-a2dc-4dae-9bdf-e97139a976c6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8de336a9417d89af","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-yaps0um6/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic applies the structured reconciliation log convention to the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary — the companion binary that handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; the remaining work is converting message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths of the three cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine), producing a coverage checklist.\n- Convert every call site that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs (the kind is not implied by the shared reconciler, so this is a functional requirement here, not just a queryability enhancement).\n- Add unit tests that parse test logger output and assert field presence and correctness for each controller.\n- Perform a before/after log output comparison confirming the production log consumer stack on cloud deployments (e.g., CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Unit tests assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are present with correct values in test logger output for each of the three cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n- The audit checklist is complete: zero `log.Error` call sites with resource name or namespace embedded in message text remain in cloudmanager reconciliation error paths.\n- A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P0): all cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Cloudmanager is deployed only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), so its impact scope is narrower than the manager binary's. Whether this work lands in the same release as the manager-binary conversion or in a subsequent release is a PM / ODH Operator team scheduling decision; it does not change the work itself, which is why it is planned as an independently schedulable unit.\n- The shared reconciler already provides `name`/`namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger, so no key-name migration is needed here — only embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind`.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":54181,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bcd7d7142debc9e","agent_id":"acc070392a2e429ac","session_id":"a719d0dc-396c-46d9-8e0e-5a6d9af711e0","uuid":"59c9ad1e-ab5b-4024-b921-ecf0e36d904a","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:18:21.743Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
