{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8580d0e6da79c3de","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Assessment\n15\t\n16\tThe strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), and a single team does the code work — but the below-threshold path does **not** apply: the scope spans two architecturally distinct work streams that warrant separate PR review cycles. (1) Mechanical code conversion across two binaries (manager and cloudmanager), and (2) substantive content authoring — a new contribution guideline documenting the structured log convention (HLR-8), which is new documentation, not an incidental touch-up falling out of the code change. Per Step 0, multiple distinct work streams proceed to full decomposition.\n17\t\n18\t## Component Mapping\n19\t\n20\t- Strategy component `rhods-operator` is not a canonical RHAI Jira component name. All epics are mapped to **OpenShift AI Productization**, the closest canonical component covering the RHOAI operator codebase (`red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator`). Logged here because `submit.py` strips non-canonical component values.\n21\t- Each epic's scope spans the manager and cloudmanager binaries of that one component; the binary split is an architectural sub-system boundary inside the component, reflected in the epic split below.\n22\t\n23\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n24\t\n25\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n26\t\n27\t1. **Action file count corrected**: 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), not \"~16 component action files\". The module controller action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) are in scope per SME confirmation that they contain error logs.\n28\t2. **Controller architecture corrected to 5 tiers**: DSC controller, 8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSCI controller, 5 service controllers (including Setup). The AI text's \"four controller tiers\" omitted the module controller tier.\n29\t3. **camelCase field naming** (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) adopted per SME specification.\n30\t4. **Cloudmanager** confirmed to use the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger — only embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind` are needed there.\n31\t\n32\t## Epic List\n33\t\n34\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n35\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator manager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document the structured reconciliation log convention and publish for external adoption | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 manager binary log standardization] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 convention guideline]\n45\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 cloudmanager log standardization] --> E003\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\t## DAG Justification\n49\t\n50\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n51\t|------|------|-----------|\n52\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics — the guideline documents the convention as implemented, so it must follow the code work. Docs edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n53\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same — the guideline also covers the cloudmanager controllers' convention. |\n54\t| (no edge) E001 ↔ E002 | Rules 14/15 | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries in the same repo with no shared artifacts — different areas, no build-against relationship. The conversion in each binary is independent; merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. They run in parallel. |\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n59\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n60\t| HLR-1: Convert message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001 | Manager-binary call sites; cloudmanager embedded-identity sites are converted in E002 (same HLR, binary split). |\n61\t| HLR-2: DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller. |\n62\t| HLR-3: Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n63\t| HLR-4: All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E001 | |\n64\t| HLR-5: Cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n65\t| HLR-6: Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n66\t| HLR-7: DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E001 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: it edits the very same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR-1/HLR-4 conversion in the same controllers. Splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit; the `resourceKind` field is added in the same mechanical pass. |\n67\t| HLR-8: Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | |\n68\t\n69\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n70\t\n71\t## Open Questions Disposition\n72\t\n73\tNo Investigation epics were created — none of the strategy's open questions changes which epics exist or what they do:\n74\t\n75\t- **Additional fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (PM / Observability): additive to the same call sites; captured as a decision point on the manager implementation epic. Does not change approach.\n76\t- **Which alerts rely on message-string patterns** (SRE / Support): resolved by the log-consumer regression validation step — an acceptance criterion on the implementation epics, not a structural unknown.\n77\t- **Cloudmanager same-release vs. subsequent release** (PM / ODH Operator team): scheduling, not structure. Cloudmanager is a separate epic (E002) precisely so it can be scheduled independently; the work exists either way.\n78\t- **Actual percentage of embedded-identity log calls** (ODH Operator team): the audit is the first step of the implementation epics' scope; the conversion proceeds over whatever sites are found.\n79\t\n80\t## Health Warnings\n81\t\n82\t- **Epic size overage (E001), kept deliberately**: The manager-binary scope (audit + conversion + DSCI migration + `resourceKind` across 5 controller tiers and 15 action files + CI lint rule) is estimated at 1.25–1.5 sprints, above the ~2-week guideline. It is kept as one epic because it is a single mechanical pass over one surface (the `log.Error` call sites) that shares one audit; splitting would duplicate the audit and fragment the pass. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along controller-tier lines (e.g., controllers vs. action files, or the DSCI key migration as its own epic) if the call-site audit finds materially more embedded-identity sites than the 75% SME estimate implies, or discovers reconciliation error paths beyond the enumerated controllers and 15 action files.\n83\t- **Pending process gates**: all strategy-level prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support, etc.) are marked \"Pending human review\". These gate execution start, not decomposition structure; the implementing team should not begin until they clear.\n84\t- **Priority bundling**: HLR-7 (P1) is bundled into the P0 manager epic — see the traceability row above for the inline rationale (same call sites, same mechanical pass).\n85\t- **External adoption is voluntary**: COO team adoption of the convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is out of RHOAI's control per platform policy (overlay 0008). Publishing and filing tracking issues is in scope (E003); adoption itself is best-effort and appears in no epic's acceptance criteria.\n86\t\n87\t## Tiered Delivery\n88\t\n89\tNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO adoption) is voluntary and non-blocking by platform policy — no downstream work is gated on it, so there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and already exists.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0910200606dfcc7","agent_id":"ad742ce4c46929491","session_id":"a719d0dc-396c-46d9-8e0e-5a6d9af711e0","uuid":"cc6fa859-9843-4c22-8cc0-6f1dd6084e0c","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:20:09.389Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8580d0e6da79c3de","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
