l pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 3-epic output is expected and is not penalized. No conditional branch files exist, and none are required — the strategy's open questions are all dispositioned without structural forks.\n37\t\n38\tThe decomposition is faithful to the strategy, applies the Staff Engineer overrides (15 action files across 4 tiers, 5 controller tiers including the module controller, camelCase keys, cloudmanager shared-reconciler status), and draws clean binary/docs boundaries. All issues found are minor; none forces a criterion to zero.\n39\t\n40\t## Criterion Details\n41\t\n42\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n43\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1/2/3/4/6/7 → E001, HLR-5 → E002, HLR-8 → E003. Traceability matrix present and consistent with each epic's HLR Traceability section. Priority inheritance is sound: E001 and E002 are P0 and block only the docs epic; E003 is `docs-authoring` and takes P1 from the strategy's Jira priority (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring exemption — its Rule 11 dependencies on P0 implementations are not violations. The HLR-7 (P1) bundling into the P0 manager epic is justified, not priority collapse: `resourceKind` on DSC/component controllers is added to the very same `log.Error` call sites being converted for HLR-1/HLR-4 in the same mechanical pass, so it is incidental polish on the P0 work rather than a deferrable feature — deferral would require re-touching every call site. The bundling is explicitly documented in the traceability row and Health Warnings.\n44\t\n45\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n46\tThe DAG is E001 → E003 and E002 → E003, matching E003's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, with arrows drawn dependency → dependent per convention. Both edges are Rule 11 (all implementations → docs-authoring) and correctly do not trigger priority inheritance. E001 ↔ E002 parallelism is correct per Rules 14/15 (separate binaries, no build-against relationship, no shared artifact). No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not implicated. Critical path length 2 fits the S-size heuristic (1-2). No missing data/artifact edges found in epic content. One coordination hazard noted as a minor issue: E001's scope adds a CI lint rule explicitly covering **both** binaries, while E002 converts the cloudmanager call sites in parallel — if the lint lands before E002's conversions merge, CI fails on cloudmanager's unconverted sites. This is merge-order coordination within one repo (not a DAG edge — the correct fixes are scoping the lint to the manager binary in E001, or sequencing its cloudmanager enablement with E002), but it slightly undercuts the decomposition's own \"no shared artifacts\" parallel justification and should be called out for the implementing team.\n47\t\n48\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n49\tAll epics are component `OpenShift AI Productization` / ODH Operator team, with the non-canonical `rhods-operator` mapping logged transparently in the summary. The E001/E002 split along binary lines is a sound sub-system boundary that makes the cloudmanager work independently schedulable (directly addressing the strategy's open question), not a team-boundary violation. No scope duplication between siblings: E002 does not rebuild any of E001's deliverables, and the lint rule exists only in E001. No work-product mixing — E003 is cleanly separated as `docs-authoring` content authoring while E001/E002 are pure code. E001's size overage (1.25–1.5 sprints) is handled exactly per the epic-size carve-out: it is one mechanical pass over one surface (the `log.Error` call sites) sharing one audit, and the Health Warning records a concrete, measurable re-split trigger (tier-line split if the audit finds materially more sites than the 75% estimate or discovers paths beyond the enumerated controllers and 15 action files). A triggered, justified overage — criterion scores 2.\n50\t\n51\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n52\tNo Investigation epics, and the disposition is correct: none of the strategy's four open questions changes which epics exist or what they do (additive field decision, alert-rule identification via validation AC, release scheduling, audit percentage confirmed by the epic's own first step). All three epics are Implementation and produce concrete artifacts (code changes + lint rule + unit tests; code changes + audit checklist; published guideline + migration guide + filed tracking issues). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set, and none are needed — no true gates exist in the DAG.\n53\t\n54\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n55\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: all three epics are Implementation with `ai_signals` populated and `investigation_signals: null`. Rationales are present and substantive, and frontmatter values match them. Two arguable/inaccurate points keep this at 1 rather than 2:\n56\t\n57\t- **E002 `human_process_gates: 0` is inconsistent with E001's `-1`** on identical strategy-level conditions. E001's rationale counts the pending strategy-level process gates (Architecture Review, Licence Validation, etc.) toward `-1`; E002's rationale argues only that the SRE alert-rule gate \"sits on the manager-binary work\" and does not address the same pending strategy gates, which per the summary's own Health Warnings gate execution start for all epics. Either E002 should also be `-1`, or the rationale should justify per-epic gate scoping explicitly.\n58\t- **E001's `architecture_claims` rationale is partially overbroad**: it asserts the action-file architecture (component/service/DSC/module handlers) \"all match the context file\", but `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md` documents three controller tiers and three service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) with zero mentions of action files, the module controller tier, or CertConfigMapGenerator/Setup. Those specifics rest on the Staff Engineer input (which legitimately trumps the AI-generated text), not on the context file. The score of 1 is still correct — controller-runtime v0.24.1, logr, `cmd/cloudmanager`, the three KubernetesEngine CRDs, DSCI, and the DSC/component/service structure are all corroborated — but the rationale should attribute the action-file/module-tier claims to the SME input rather than the context file.\n59\t\n60\tE001's `open_questions: 0` is defensible: the PM field-extension question is additive to the same call sites and the rationale addresses each open question individually.\n61\t\n62\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n63\tAll three epics have concrete, testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria (lint-rule zero-flag, unit tests parsing test logger output per controller and per action file, grep for old DSCI keys, before/after ingestion comparison). Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E003 (`docs-authoring`) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; no konflux chain exists; this is not a component replacement so no rollback/feature-flag AC is mandated. External adoption by the COO team is correctly kept out of the ACs (E003's AC is on publishing and filing tracking issues, which are in the epic's control). No draft-ID references to sibling epics appear in any epic body. One minor misfiled-AC: E001's final AC bundles an externally-controlled merge gate — \"text-based alert rules identified with SRE/Support are updated or confirmed unaffected **before merge**\" — the alert-rule-update half depends on SRE/Support action and turnaround. The before/after verification belongs in the ACs; the external-team update dependency belongs in Risks/Notes (where it already appears) rather than as a merge-blocking AC.\n64\t\n65\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n66\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all five controller tiers plus cloudmanager in E001/E002, the 15 action files across 4 tiers, DSCI key migration, cluster-scoped